  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "For the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}+72x-100y+19=0$, the length of the major axis is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}+72x-100y+19 & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+72x)+(25{y}^{2}-100y)+19 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+72x)+(25{y}^{2}-100y)+19 & =0 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}+8x]+25[{y}^{2}-4y]+19 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+25[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+19 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+4)^{2}-16]+25[(y-2)^{2}-4]+19 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+4)^{2}-144+25(y-2)^{2}-100+19 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x+4)^{2}-144+25(y-2)^{2}-100+19 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+4)^{2}+25(y-2)^{2} & =-19+100+144 \\\\ 9(x+4)^{2}+25(y-2)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5=10.$",
